1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,380
Your CTO forwards an alert and asks:
"We put .env in .gitignore months ago.

2
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:13,972
Why did the secret scanner find the
key in the repository last night?"

3
00:00:14,312 --> 00:00:18,866
Two words first. The dot env file is
a local file of settings and secrets.

4
00:00:18,906 --> 00:00:23,833
The dot gitignore file lists patterns for
files that Git should leave out of its

5
00:00:23,873 --> 00:00:24,391
listings.

6
00:00:24,731 --> 00:00:29,702
The .gitignore is correct. The pattern
matches. And the file has been in every

7
00:00:29,742 --> 00:00:32,272
commit since. How can all three be true?

8
00:00:34,612 --> 00:00:39,814
This is the eighth most-voted Git question
on Stack Overflow, with 8,647 votes on

9
00:00:39,854 --> 00:00:45,121
the first of October 2026 according to the
course's research report: how do I make

10
00:00:45,161 --> 00:00:49,458
Git forget about a file that was
tracked, but is now in .gitignore?

11
00:00:49,498 --> 00:00:53,082
So if this has bitten you,
you're in very large company.

12
00:00:53,122 --> 00:00:58,454
It has one cause, and you can already state
it from the last video's decision tree:

13
00:00:58,494 --> 00:01:02,532
an ignore rule never applies to
a path that is already tracked.

14
00:01:04,372 --> 00:01:07,852
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:07,892 --> 00:01:12,585
This video has two halves. First, how ignore
rules work: where patterns come from,

16
00:01:12,625 --> 00:01:17,376
in which order they win, what the syntax
means, and the one command that debugs any

17
00:01:17,416 --> 00:01:22,166
of it. Second, the trap: the tracked file
that an ignore rule can't touch, the fix,

18
00:01:22,206 --> 00:01:26,957
and the two things the fix doesn't do. Those
two things have each caused incidents,

19
00:01:26,997 --> 00:01:29,727
and they're the production
lesson of this video.

20
00:01:30,067 --> 00:01:34,759
One reminder from the last video: tracked
means the path has an entry in the index,

21
00:01:34,799 --> 00:01:37,495
Git's list of what the
next commit will contain.

22
00:01:39,335 --> 00:01:40,913
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:41,253 --> 00:01:42,666
After this video you can:

24
00:01:43,006 --> 00:01:46,068
List the sources of ignore
patterns in precedence order.

25
00:01:46,408 --> 00:01:49,578
Find the rule that ignores a
given path with one command.

26
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:55,075
Explain why adding a tracked file to
.gitignore changes nothing, and fix it.

27
00:01:55,415 --> 00:01:59,612
State what the fix does not do to
history and to teammates' working trees.

28
00:02:01,452 --> 00:02:06,176
In one sentence. An ignore pattern tells
the commands that scan the working tree for

29
00:02:06,216 --> 00:02:08,671
untracked paths to leave
matching paths out.

30
00:02:09,011 --> 00:02:12,883
Four sources, in precedence
order. From highest to lowest.

31
00:02:13,223 --> 00:02:19,297
First, command-line options, for commands
that take them, for example git clean -e.

32
00:02:19,337 --> 00:02:22,612
Scope: one command. Use:
a one-off exception.

33
00:02:22,952 --> 00:02:27,106
Second, .gitignore in the path's
directory or any parent directory.

34
00:02:27,146 --> 00:02:32,177
A deeper file overrides a shallower one.
It's a tracked file, so it's shared with

35
00:02:32,217 --> 00:02:37,060
the team. Use it for what every developer
should ignore: build output, caches,

36
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:38,563
datasets, secrets files.

37
00:02:38,903 --> 00:02:43,432
Third, .git/info/exclude.
Scope: this clone. Not shared.

38
00:02:43,472 --> 00:02:47,756
Use it for your own scratch
files in this repository.

39
00:02:48,096 --> 00:02:53,101
Fourth, the file named by
core.excludesFile. Its default is

40
00:02:53,141 --> 00:03:00,199
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore, or
~/.config/git/ignore when that variable is not set.

41
00:03:00,239 --> 00:03:06,955
Scope: every repository on this machine.
Use it for editor and operating-system

42
00:03:06,995 --> 00:03:09,947
noise such as .DS_Store and .idea/.

43
00:03:10,287 --> 00:03:13,148
Within one source, the last
matching pattern decides.

44
00:03:13,488 --> 00:03:14,543
Pattern syntax.

45
00:03:14,883 --> 00:03:20,246
A pattern with no slash, or only a trailing
slash, matches at any depth below the

46
00:03:20,286 --> 00:03:25,716
.gitignore: *.log matches logs/app.log. A
trailing slash matches directories only:

47
00:03:25,756 --> 00:03:30,919
__pycache__/. A slash at the start or in
the middle anchors the pattern to the

48
00:03:30,959 --> 00:03:35,722
directory of the .gitignore: /build/
matches build/, and not src/build/.

49
00:03:35,762 --> 00:03:39,658
The wildcards *, ? and bracket
ranges do not match a slash.

50
00:03:39,698 --> 00:03:44,727
Two asterisks match any number of
directories. A leading exclamation mark is

51
00:03:44,767 --> 00:03:49,063
negation: it re-includes a path
that an earlier pattern excluded.

52
00:03:49,103 --> 00:03:50,931
A leading hash is a comment.

53
00:03:51,271 --> 00:03:56,344
That's a lot of syntax in one breath. In
the demo, each of those rules decides a

54
00:03:56,384 --> 00:03:56,983
real path.

55
00:03:57,323 --> 00:04:02,195
The limit of negation. The manual states
that "it is not possible to re-include a

56
00:04:02,235 --> 00:04:06,926
file if a parent directory of that file
is excluded", because Git doesn't look

57
00:04:06,966 --> 00:04:12,021
inside an excluded directory at all. So to
keep one file inside an otherwise ignored

58
00:04:12,061 --> 00:04:16,752
directory, exclude the directory's
contents, data/*, not the directory, data/.

59
00:04:17,092 --> 00:04:22,664
Inside .git. Ignore rules leave no trace
in the index or the object database.

60
00:04:22,704 --> 00:04:28,640
.gitignore is an ordinary tracked file.
.git/info/exclude is a plain file that git

61
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:34,616
init creates with a few comment lines.
Neither is consulted for a path that has an

62
00:04:34,656 --> 00:04:35,491
index entry.

63
00:04:35,831 --> 00:04:40,767
The trap. That last sentence is the trap.
Ignore patterns are consulted only for

64
00:04:40,807 --> 00:04:45,681
paths that have no index entry: when
status lists untracked files, and when add

65
00:04:45,721 --> 00:04:49,849
walks a directory. A tracked path
is compared with its index entry.

66
00:04:49,889 --> 00:04:55,074
No pattern is checked. So when you edit a
tracked dot env file, status reports it as

67
00:04:55,114 --> 00:05:00,174
modified, whatever the ignore file says.
If the file was tracked first and ignored

68
00:05:00,214 --> 00:05:01,543
second, tracking wins.

69
00:05:01,883 --> 00:05:07,037
Why does Git do this? The manual defines
the purpose of ignore files narrowly: "to

70
00:05:07,077 --> 00:05:11,028
ensure that certain files not
tracked by Git remain untracked".

71
00:05:11,068 --> 00:05:15,399
What is tracked changes only when a
command says so: git add, git rm.

72
00:05:15,739 --> 00:05:20,966
The fix. git rm --cached <path>, then
commit, and keep the pattern in .gitignore.

73
00:05:21,006 --> 00:05:26,102
git rm --cached is 🟡 CAUTION: it removes
the index entry and leaves the file on

74
00:05:26,142 --> 00:05:30,133
disk. The next commit deletes
the path for everyone who pulls.

75
00:05:30,173 --> 00:05:35,334
And treat any secret in the file as leaked,
and rotate it: replace it with a new

76
00:05:35,374 --> 00:05:36,960
one, and disable the old.

77
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:41,712
What the fix does not do. First, it
doesn't remove the file from history.

78
00:05:41,752 --> 00:05:46,226
Every earlier commit still contains
it. If the file held a credential, the

79
00:05:46,266 --> 00:05:49,947
credential is in every clone
and, once pushed, on the server.

80
00:05:49,987 --> 00:05:54,582
The order of operations for a leaked
secret is to revoke or rotate it first.

81
00:05:54,622 --> 00:05:59,523
Second, the fix is a deletion commit, and
Git applies deletions to working trees.

82
00:05:59,563 --> 00:06:04,342
A teammate who cloned while the file was
tracked loses her copy when she pulls.

83
00:06:04,682 --> 00:06:09,503
Ignored means expendable. A third consequence,
for ignored files in general: Git

84
00:06:09,543 --> 00:06:13,635
treats an ignored file as something
it may overwrite without asking.

85
00:06:13,675 --> 00:06:18,739
If the same path is ignored on one branch
and tracked on another, switching branches

86
00:06:18,779 --> 00:06:23,783
replaces your ignored file, silently. An
untracked file in the way normally stops a

87
00:06:23,823 --> 00:06:24,329
checkout.

88
00:06:24,669 --> 00:06:30,030
An ignored file doesn't: --overwrite-ignore
is the default for checkout, switch and

89
00:06:30,070 --> 00:06:35,430
merge. The textbook labels that case 🔴
DANGEROUS. The prevention is to give private

90
00:06:35,470 --> 00:06:40,635
files a name that no commit on any branch
has ever tracked, or to use git switch

91
00:06:40,675 --> 00:06:43,888
--no-overwrite-ignore when
moving to old branches.

92
00:06:44,228 --> 00:06:49,702
When not to use .gitignore. Not as protection
for secrets: it's advice to git status

93
00:06:49,742 --> 00:06:54,953
and git add, nothing more. It doesn't stop
git add -f, and it does nothing about

94
00:06:54,993 --> 00:07:00,401
history. Not for files the team must share.
And not for "ignore my local edits to a

95
00:07:00,441 --> 00:07:05,914
tracked file": Git has no such feature.
Video 18 covers the two index bits that look

96
00:07:05,954 --> 00:07:06,505
like one.

97
00:07:08,345 --> 00:07:12,743
The textbook's analogy: a "do not list"
note taped to the warehouse door for the

98
00:07:12,783 --> 00:07:16,071
people doing the audit. It
changes what the auditors report.

99
00:07:16,111 --> 00:07:20,397
It doesn't move, lock or hide anything,
and it has no effect on items that are

100
00:07:20,437 --> 00:07:21,784
already in the inventory.

101
00:07:22,124 --> 00:07:27,207
That last clause is where most people's
mental model breaks, so if yours did, that's

102
00:07:27,247 --> 00:07:31,109
normal. They read .gitignore as
"Git, do not touch these files".

103
00:07:31,149 --> 00:07:35,134
It's narrower: "when you look for
new things to list, skip these".

104
00:07:35,174 --> 00:07:38,732
A file that is already in the
inventory is not a new thing.

105
00:07:39,072 --> 00:07:43,733
And where the analogy itself breaks down:
the note isn't neutral for the items it

106
00:07:43,773 --> 00:07:48,550
covers. Things under the note are treated
as expendable, as you heard a moment ago.

107
00:07:48,590 --> 00:07:52,033
An auditor wouldn't throw them
away. Git may overwrite them.

108
00:07:53,873 --> 00:07:56,883
On the "yes" branch the
ignore rules are never read.

109
00:07:56,923 --> 00:07:59,053
That's the whole trap in one picture.

110
00:08:10,093 --> 00:08:12,611
The demo's .gitignore, with line numbers.

111
00:08:12,951 --> 00:08:16,180
git check-ignore -v is the
debugger for ignore rules.

112
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:20,930
For each path it prints the source file,
the line number and the pattern that

113
00:08:20,970 --> 00:08:25,865
decided. Try it now, before the output.
Pause, take thirty seconds, and for each

114
00:08:25,905 --> 00:08:29,443
path write your answer: ignored
or not, and by which line?

115
00:08:34,283 --> 00:08:39,505
Read it line by line. /build/ on line
6 ignores the top-level build/ and not

116
00:08:39,545 --> 00:08:43,520
src/build/helper.py, because
the leading slash anchors it.

117
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:47,535
With -n, a path that matches
no pattern prints two colons.

118
00:08:47,575 --> 00:08:51,066
.env.example matched one
line and then a later one.

119
00:08:51,106 --> 00:08:56,743
The later line wins, and because that line
is a negation, the path is not ignored.

120
00:08:56,783 --> 00:09:02,420
A match on a ! pattern means "not ignored".
So read the pattern, not only the fact

121
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:03,874
that there is output.

122
00:09:04,214 --> 00:09:05,848
Status agrees with the debugger.

123
00:09:06,188 --> 00:09:10,669
Now the limit of negation. The
script changes data/* to data/.

124
00:09:10,709 --> 00:09:16,648
The exception line for data/README.md stays.
Predict: is the README still visible?

125
00:09:16,688 --> 00:09:18,617
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

126
00:09:23,457 --> 00:09:28,560
No. The exception line is still there, and
it no longer has any effect, because the

127
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:32,278
directory itself is excluded
and Git doesn't look inside it.

128
00:09:32,318 --> 00:09:34,757
Almost everyone writes it this way once.

129
00:09:35,097 --> 00:09:40,137
Precedence between files: a .gitignore
deeper in the tree overrides the ones above

130
00:09:40,177 --> 00:09:41,562
it, for paths below it.

131
00:09:41,902 --> 00:09:46,653
The two personal sources. They
never travel with the repository.

132
00:09:46,693 --> 00:09:50,546
In the lab, XDG_CONFIG_HOME
points into the sandbox.

133
00:09:50,586 --> 00:09:55,337
On your machine the default global
file is ~/.config/git/ignore.

134
00:09:55,377 --> 00:09:58,032
The debugger names each source file.

135
00:09:58,372 --> 00:10:04,138
Quick quiz, two options. My personal file
ignores every notebook, with *.ipynb.

136
00:10:04,178 --> 00:10:10,018
The repository's own .gitignore re-includes
one of them, notebooks/report.ipynb.

137
00:10:10,058 --> 00:10:14,942
Who wins? Option one: my personal
file. Option two: the repository.

138
00:10:14,982 --> 00:10:16,118
Say it out loud.

139
00:10:20,958 --> 00:10:26,193
Option two. The repository outranks both
personal files, so a project can insist on

140
00:10:26,233 --> 00:10:29,689
tracking something that one
developer ignores globally.

141
00:10:30,029 --> 00:10:34,214
Finally, git add -f 🟢 SAFE
overrides the rules for one path.

142
00:10:34,254 --> 00:10:37,312
Ignore rules are advice
for untracked paths.

143
00:10:40,152 --> 00:10:44,408
The mistake: the first commit of
a project is made with git add .

144
00:10:44,448 --> 00:10:47,779
before any .gitignore exists,
so .env is committed.

145
00:10:48,119 --> 00:10:53,918
The rule arrives one commit later. .env is
listed in .gitignore, and still shows as

146
00:10:53,958 --> 00:10:58,772
modified. Now diagnose. Predict what
git check-ignore -v .env prints.

147
00:11:03,612 --> 00:11:08,816
Nothing, with exit status 1: ignore rules
are not consulted for a tracked path.

148
00:11:08,856 --> 00:11:13,928
--no-index asks the same question with
the index left out, and shows that the

149
00:11:13,968 --> 00:11:19,372
pattern itself is fine. The last command
is the detector to remember: git ls-files

150
00:11:19,412 --> 00:11:24,616
--cached --ignored --exclude-standard
lists every path that is tracked and also

151
00:11:24,656 --> 00:11:29,197
matches an ignore pattern. In a
healthy repository it prints nothing.

152
00:11:29,537 --> 00:11:32,128
The fix: git rm --cached 🟡 CAUTION.

153
00:11:32,468 --> 00:11:37,311
Between the rm and the commit, status shows
the path twice: D in the first column,

154
00:11:37,351 --> 00:11:42,194
because the index no longer has what HEAD
has. And !!, because the file on disk is

155
00:11:42,234 --> 00:11:44,873
now an untracked path
that matches a pattern.

156
00:11:45,213 --> 00:11:50,109
After the commit, the file is still on disk
with your content, and later edits are

157
00:11:50,149 --> 00:11:53,420
invisible to status. That
looks like the end. It isn't.

158
00:11:53,460 --> 00:11:56,309
Predict: is the secret
gone from the repository?

159
00:12:01,149 --> 00:12:04,833
Stop here. The fix changed
the index and the new commit.

160
00:12:04,873 --> 00:12:09,688
It didn't change any earlier commit.
git show HEAD~1:.env prints the key.

161
00:12:10,028 --> 00:12:14,507
Here's the history as a picture: three
commits, with the label main on the newest

162
00:12:14,547 --> 00:12:18,858
one, the fix. Removing the path from the
next commit changes nothing about the

163
00:12:18,898 --> 00:12:21,202
commits before it. Revoke or rotate first.

164
00:12:21,542 --> 00:12:27,010
Second stop. Asha cloned while .env was
still tracked, and her local runs read that

165
00:12:27,050 --> 00:12:32,053
file. She pulls: she fetches your new
commit and brings her branch up to it.

166
00:12:35,393 --> 00:12:40,919
Her copy is gone. From Git's point of view
this is correct: the path was tracked in

167
00:12:40,959 --> 00:12:46,351
her HEAD, unmodified, and is absent from
the new commit, so it's removed like any

168
00:12:46,391 --> 00:12:51,783
other deleted file. She can take the content
back out of the previous commit with

169
00:12:51,823 --> 00:12:53,996
git restore --source=HEAD~1 .env.

170
00:12:54,336 --> 00:12:58,937
The file is then untracked and ignored,
which is the state you wanted for everyone.

171
00:12:58,977 --> 00:13:03,355
If she had edited her .env, the pull would
have stopped with a message that her

172
00:13:03,395 --> 00:13:07,941
local changes would be overwritten. So
announce a fix of this kind before you push

173
00:13:07,981 --> 00:13:10,345
it, with those two
commands in the message.

174
00:13:10,685 --> 00:13:15,950
And the third consequence. Your .env is
ignored now, and holds a value that exists

175
00:13:15,990 --> 00:13:20,737
nowhere else. The branch release-1.0
was cut while .env was still tracked.

176
00:13:20,777 --> 00:13:26,041
Switching to the old branch replaced your
file with the tracked version, silently.

177
00:13:26,081 --> 00:13:30,570
Switching back deleted it. Your value
is gone, and no object holds it.

178
00:13:30,610 --> 00:13:32,769
Twig looks worried, and fairly so.

179
00:13:34,609 --> 00:13:36,257
Five mistakes to watch for.

180
00:13:36,597 --> 00:13:40,642
Adding a tracked file to .gitignore
and expecting Git to forget it.

181
00:13:40,682 --> 00:13:45,640
Root cause: the file was tracked first and
ignored second, and ignore patterns are

182
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:48,567
consulted only for paths
without an index entry.

183
00:13:48,907 --> 00:13:53,941
A ! exception has no effect. Root cause:
the parent directory is excluded, data/

184
00:13:53,981 --> 00:13:58,382
instead of data/*, and Git does not
look inside an excluded directory.

185
00:13:58,722 --> 00:14:02,588
Believing that git rm --cached
plus a commit removed the secret.

186
00:14:02,628 --> 00:14:07,714
Root cause: the fix changes the index and
the new commit; every earlier commit still

187
00:14:07,754 --> 00:14:08,812
contains the file.

188
00:14:09,152 --> 00:14:13,698
A teammate's local file vanishes after git
pull. Root cause: the pull contained a

189
00:14:13,738 --> 00:14:18,339
commit that stopped tracking the file, and
Git applies deletions to working trees.

190
00:14:18,679 --> 00:14:23,803
Keeping an irreplaceable value in an ignored
file whose path another branch tracks.

191
00:14:23,843 --> 00:14:28,904
Root cause: ignored files are assumed to
be regenerable, and --overwrite-ignore is

192
00:14:28,944 --> 00:14:31,579
the default for checkout,
switch and merge.

193
00:14:33,419 --> 00:14:39,028
Now, out of the lab. For an AI and ML
repository, the shared .gitignore usually

194
00:14:39,068 --> 00:14:43,677
starts from a language template
and adds what the template lacks.

195
00:14:43,717 --> 00:14:49,326
The textbook notes that GitHub's Python
template covers interpreter, packaging,

196
00:14:49,366 --> 00:14:54,546
environment and tool caches and has
nothing specific to machine learning.

197
00:14:54,586 --> 00:15:00,410
The usual additions it lists: data directories.
Checkpoint and export formats such

198
00:15:00,450 --> 00:15:04,487
as *.pt, *.pth, *.ckpt,
*.safetensors, *.onnx and *.gguf.

199
00:15:04,527 --> 00:15:08,277
Experiment-tracker output
such as wandb/ and mlruns/.

200
00:15:08,317 --> 00:15:14,141
And .env files. Editor and operating-system
noise stays in each developer's global

201
00:15:14,181 --> 00:15:14,999
ignore file.

202
00:15:15,339 --> 00:15:20,753
Two rules keep the file honest. Put a
pattern in the shared file only if every

203
00:15:20,793 --> 00:15:26,278
developer and CI should ignore the path.
And never treat the file as a security

204
00:15:26,318 --> 00:15:31,592
control. The prevention for the trap
itself is one line in CI: fail when git

205
00:15:31,632 --> 00:15:34,949
ls-files -ci --exclude-standard
prints anything.

206
00:15:36,789 --> 00:15:42,219
Your turn. Do Lab 2.3, "The
.gitignore trap and its fix", in

207
00:15:42,259 --> 00:15:46,047
lab-manual/m02-working-tree-index-head.md.

208
00:15:46,387 --> 00:15:51,279
Before the diagnosis step, predict
the output and the exit status of git

209
00:15:51,319 --> 00:15:56,621
check-ignore -v for the tracked file,
and of the same command with --no-index.

210
00:15:56,661 --> 00:16:02,305
Before the fix, predict what git status
--short --ignored will show between the git

211
00:16:02,345 --> 00:16:04,154
rm --cached and the commit.

212
00:16:05,994 --> 00:16:11,904
Q45: "A developer added config/secrets.yaml
to .gitignore and it is still in every

213
00:16:11,944 --> 00:16:17,781
new commit. Walk through your diagnosis
commands, the fix, and the two things the

214
00:16:17,821 --> 00:16:19,233
fix does not solve."

215
00:16:21,573 --> 00:16:26,491
Answer out loud. A strong answer follows
the framework: it states the mechanism

216
00:16:26,531 --> 00:16:31,450
before any fix, names the commands that
confirm it, and labels the fix with its

217
00:16:31,490 --> 00:16:36,535
risk. Then it spends as long on the two
unsolved problems as on the fix, and says

218
00:16:36,575 --> 00:16:40,175
what has to happen first when
the file holds a credential.

219
00:16:42,015 --> 00:16:47,327
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: ignore patterns come from four

220
00:16:47,367 --> 00:16:52,953
sources: command-line options, .gitignore
files, .git/info/exclude and my personal

221
00:16:52,993 --> 00:16:58,716
excludes file, in that order of precedence,
and within a source the last match wins.

222
00:16:58,756 --> 00:17:03,244
git check-ignore -v names the file,
line and pattern that decided.

223
00:17:03,284 --> 00:17:08,527
Ignore rules apply only to paths with
no index entry, so a tracked file stays

224
00:17:08,567 --> 00:17:09,076
tracked.

225
00:17:09,416 --> 00:17:14,114
git rm --cached plus a commit stops
tracking it. That doesn't remove it from

226
00:17:14,154 --> 00:17:18,790
history, and it deletes the file in the
working tree of everyone who pulls.

227
00:17:18,830 --> 00:17:23,217
And an ignored file is expendable:
Git may overwrite it without asking.

228
00:17:23,557 --> 00:17:28,781
And the CTO's question? The rule was right.
The file was tracked before the rule

229
00:17:28,821 --> 00:17:33,913
existed, so the rule never applied to
it, and the key has been in history ever

230
00:17:33,953 --> 00:17:34,308
since.

